          "title": "You'll want to know: formīdō, formīdinis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "formīdō, formīdinis — \"dread, terror\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Magna formīdō mīlitēs tenēbat. Great dread held the soldiers."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, 3rd declension. There is also a verb formīdō, formīdāre, 'to dread'; English formidable comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "formīdō is explained by Lewis & Short as \"the fear that makes one rigid\", from a root also seen in firmus, \"firm\"; the link is not certain. English formidable comes from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
