          "title": "You'll want to know: trepidō, trepidāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trepidō, trepidāre — \"to panic, to be in a flurry\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cīvēs in viīs trepidābant, nec quid facerent sciēbant. The citizens were rushing about in alarm in the streets and did not know what to do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trepidō, trepidāre, trepidāvī, trepidātum: anxious hurrying, the bustle of fear. English trepidation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trepidō is built on trepidus, \"restless, alarmed\" (EtymDB). English trepidation comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
